.TH "DEDB RUN" "1" "2026-01-01" "0.0.14" "dedb run Manual"
.SH NAME
dedb\-run \- Run a game in DOSBox or DOSEMU2.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dedb run
[OPTIONS] GAME [EMULATOR_ARGS]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a game in DOSBox or DOSEMU2.
.PP
GAME is gog://<id>, archive://<id>, an archive.org URL, or a name you
have downloaded. It is downloaded, and for --dosemu converted, first
if needed. Arguments after `--` go straight to the emulator.
.PP
--cmdline prints the resulting command (a shell-pasteable line) and
stops, without running or converting anything. The game must already
be downloaded.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-dosbox\fP
Run in DOSBox.
.TP
\fB\-\-dosemu\fP
Run in DOSEMU2.
.TP
\fB\-\-profile\fP TEXT
Launch profile (gog:// only). Same as gog://<id>?profile=<slug>.
.TP
\fB\-b,\fP \-\-backend SCHEME
Read GAME as a bare id for this backend, rather than a <scheme>://<id> URL.
.TP
\fB\-\-redownload\fP
Re-download and re-extract even if already present.
.TP
\fB\-r,\fP \-\-refreshmetadata
Re-fetch cached backend metadata instead of using the cached copy.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\fP
Keep the installer/archive after extracting.
.TP
\fB\-v,\fP \-\-verbose
Print the command line before launching.
.TP
\fB\-\-cmdline\fP
Print the command --dosbox/--dosemu would run and stop (needs the game downloaded; writes nothing).
